# HPC access

Speed targets and when to start speed work: `.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`. This file is how memory is read. The layouts are named in `docs/design.ja.md`. Do not copy the rest of that spec here.

Two storages. Do not treat one as the other.

- Points are row-major `&[f64]`. Point `i` is `i * D .. (i + 1) * D`, or `chunks_exact(D)`. Coordinate `j` of that point is `points[i * D + j]`.
- `faer::Mat` is column-major. Touch it only as `(row, col)`. In the QR edge matrix, column `j` is edge `j`: row `i` holds coordinate `i` of that edge. The null direction is the last column of Q, read down that column.

A distance kernel may gather one coordinate of several points into a lane temporary. The input slice stays row-major. One point per lane, the same operations in the same order as the scalar path, and no `mul_add` unless the certified error bound includes FMA rounding. The vector path and the scalar path of that kernel return bitwise identical `w` and `l`. The SIMD scan only culls points proved strictly inside. One point's side may be proved by the same certified threshold, in either direction, never a zero sign (design §1); everything else goes to the orientation.

Do not store the points column-major, transpose them, or keep a second copy so a gather becomes contiguous. Do not walk a `Mat` buffer as rows, and do not swap `(row, col)`. Do not add an alignment, a prefetch, or an `unsafe` SIMD intrinsic the design does not name. A cache of lifted coordinates and the search cutoffs stay on their roadmap rows.

```rust
// BAD — points read as columns; faer's buffer walked as a row
let x = points[j * n + i];
let first_row = &q_buf[..ncols];

// GOOD — one point is contiguous; faer is (row, col); a gather copies one coordinate
let p = &points[i * d..(i + 1) * d];
let last_column: Vec<f64> = (0..d).map(|row| q[(row, d - 1)]).collect();
*slot = points[index as usize * d + j];
```
